The best thing about Field Service was we were wage class 3...

Hourly pay was ok...but Wage Class 3 was magic.  40 hours/wk minimum
pay with paid overtime... I sucked in all the overtime I could do -- I
was single.  I think I made more $$$ than Field Service Managers  
did by working 60-80 hrs week and pulling standby pager pay.

Get 14.5k salary with company car ($3.00/wk to cover personal use up to
300 miles/wk and insurance in the NJ no fault hell area).  I must have
made 25k the first year.  And six weeks to 15 weeks of training in the
first two years... (I volunteered every time someone turned down
time in Bedford, Ma and spent a ton of time in Princeton Park
appartments in Lowell. Both DEC and Wang rented a ton of appartments
and the disputes between loyalist were interesting.  I've heard of major
summer water fights...)

When I realize I'm working more now for less in quality of life I get
really depressed.

Now it's 40+ hours per week without overtime if things hit the fan and
pager duty isn't the money generator it used to be.

And in the old days I could do all the night and weekend installs I
could take -- since the Vaxes were selling like hotcakes and most of the
work setting up sites involved major reconfigs of drives and networks.

The good old days of Field Circus.

"Here's to folks of branches 7HT, 79J, and 78A, Mike Callahan" he said 
throwing the empty glass into the fireplace.  And to the sons of bitches 
who managed it all and ruined the industry."

Ah, Unix sysadmin and internet operations have never been as fun as a
busted RP07 at 2am at Squibb or a down 11/780 at American Cyanimid.



Bill
