You'll want to inquire over on the comp.os.vms newsgroup.

A MicroVAX does not have a graphics card or naything like that. It uses
a serial terminal for its "console". Likewise the "BIOS", or more
correctly, the console program, is CLI oriented instead of
screen-oriented with a character-cell interface.

In place of a serial terminal, you can use a terminal program on a PC.
You will need to read up on what we call "MMJ" connections ("Modified
Modular Jack", so called because it is modified from the more common
modular connectors in that the locking tab is offset to one side rather
than centered). The pinouts can be found on the web via the OpenVMS FAQ
at http://www.openvms.compaq.com/wizard/openvms_faq.html The data
rate/format will likely be 9600-N-1 (9600 baud, no parity, one stop
bit).

"CompacTape" is the predecessor to the more common "DLT" ("Digital
Linear Tape"). The drive model is probably TZ30, a SCSI-I device. The
cartridges, called TK50-K, have a native capacity of 95MB. These early
drives provided no hardware compression.

VMS has very few similarities to UNIX beyond the basics of being a
multi-processing operating system. VMS can be thought of as a super-set
of UNIX. However, discussing this in detail tens to open "operating
system wars" in the newsgroups. So, best to just read up on it and ask
questions where appropriate.

If the machine is in any condition to boot (possible, but don't take it
for granted) here are some possible scenarios, assuming you can console
terminal connected to the lowest-numbered MMJ jack:

1. The machine will power up and boot VMS. As you will likely not know
the password for the SYSTEM accound or any other, this will not be of
much use to you. See the OpenVMS FAQ (link above) for information about
how to get into a mahcine when you don't know the SYSTEM account
password or any other.

2. The machine will power up and come to the console prompt which looks
like this:

>>> 

At that point, typing HELP should provide some entertainment. (BTW, you
press <Return> after entering commands at the console. Sorry if that
sounds condescending, but you'd be surprised how many folks don't know
that.)

3. The machine will fail to power up, and may or may not display
anything on the console terminal. There are some diagnostic LEDs visible
in a small window at the rear of the unit. Note the LEDs which are lit
and whether they are on solid, flashing, etc.; then, ask about it in
comp.os.vms or comp.sys.dec or both.

A "MicroVAX" is a smaller, usually desktop-size version of the mid-range
VAX computer system line sold in the 1980s and 1990s by then Digital
Equipment Corporation. Digital was bought by Compaq circa. 1998 and
Compaq was just acquired by HP. A "VAXstation" is likewise usually a
desktop machine, but does include graphics (usually RGB, sync on green)
and does need a keyboard and usually a mouse.

The VAX CPU is a CISC machine ("Complex Instruction Set Computing"), a
32-bit processor. It was replaced in the Digital product line circa.
1992 by the 64-bit RISC ("Reduced Instruction Set Computing") Alpha
processor.

VMS ("Virtual Memory System") was written based on the VAX processor
architecture and uses many elements of earlier Digital operating systems
such as RMS ("Record Management Services") which helps user programs do
record-level I/O by eliminating the need to provide such services in the
program itself, and ODS (On-Disk Structure) which is the "filesystem".
ODS is an early predecessor to ISO-9660. It provides for file
"versioning", in that a version number is tacked onto a file
specification by the system so that you can have more than one "version"
of a file. For example, "MYFILE.DAT;1" and "MYFILE.DAT;2". The command
language is called DCL ("Digital Command Language") which is extensible
and can be very consistent from one command to the next, unlike UN*X
where a command option such as "-r" might mean one thing to "dd", but
something very different to "tar", for example. DCL has an API allowing
that command line processing can be done by system-provided services
rather than having to include such code in each user program.

I could go on, of course, but that's the basics. Hope you enjoy learning
about VMS. It's been a great environment for me to work in for the last
20 years.

