Doc,

There is nothing to prevent you from replacing the CPU chips in one or both
54-24038-01 modules to arrive at the same steppings for all CPUs.

The same steppings (more easily the same production code) guarantee that all
CPUs execute the same instructions with exactly the same timings.  Different
steppings have different microcode inside, and the microcode often introduces
subtle differences in instruction timings, leading to race conditions and other
extraordinary phenomena.  Intel has long made public statements about same
steppings in SMP systems.

I am surprised that the CPU cards have CPUs with different steppings.  DEC went
to great lengths to make sure the steppings were the same, including ordering
and carrying large amounts of inventory to be used for upgrades.

Let me suggest removing the "first" CPU module that runs the tests OK,
installing the second module in its physical place, and running AMIDIAGS on the
second module alone.  This will tell you something useful about the modules
and/or CPUs.  If the second module passes AMIDIAGS with flying colors, then you
probably have a stepping problem.  If the second module fails, the second module
or one of its component parts (cache or CPU) is faulty... Ben Myers 

