In article <<3e366f3a$0$27992$afc38c87@news.optusnet.com.au>>, Robert Symmons <robertsymmons@optushome.com.au> wrote:
>
> I don't nearly know enough about HDD logic, but I would like to try to swap
> the PCB. I will attempt to source one locally (Melbourne, Australia). If I
> do change the PCB, should I expect the partition information to be intact
> and the drive readable, or is grasping too hard on the straws?

I once had an in-house tech restore a dead drive with the critical company
data on it (and the tape drive had *just* died! later traced to another
tech doing unauthorized fiddling with tape drive jumpers and never told
anyone about it...grrr) by doing a few things -- freezing the drive, then
dropping it to the floor, then replacing the PCB. All that was just enough
to keep the drive alive long enough for us to extract the data off it!

It died for good about 30 seconds after the copy finished. :)

The tech who did unauthorized fiddling around was out of a job since his
undisclosed and unapproved monkeying around almost put us out of business.

I don't know about IDE drives, but one key thing is stored on the PCBs of
the SCSI drives: the bad block information. By swapping PCBs, that is
essentially lost. That's the only thing I'd be really careful about with
such a swap.

Sounds like you subsequently found what sounds like a virus, although the
drive should still have been at least detectable by the BIOS. Sounds like
you were unlucky to hit on both an hardware and software issue at the same
time!

-Dan


