Richard Tomkins wrote:

> Very old printers.
>
> Anyhow, probably what has happened is the pickups rollers are becoming hard.
>
> This is going to seem like a lot of work, it is. I have done this job to a
> number of printer over the years including Epsons and H-P's and it can be
> worth it.
>
> 1. Disassemble printer.
> 2. Remove the rubber compound rollers from their mounts.
> 3. Wash with soap and rinse well, all the rollers. Dry carefully. No alcohol
> of any kind.
> 4. Lightly scuff the surface with a 300 grit sandpaper, the grey stuff with
> clothe back. Probably you will need to make 6 to 8 swipes evenly across the
> entire surface of the rubber rollers.
> 5. Reassemble printers and enjoy another bunch of years use.
>
> rtt

  Even easier - using a clean soft cotton cloth, rub the rollers dry crosswise,
90 degrees from the path of the paper.  May not need to disassemble the printer
if you can reach the rollers in question.  Keep scrubbing until they return to
their original color and are slightly tacky to the touch.

  It has been my experience that the dust from the paper (and even the finest
paper has some dust) tends to impregnate the rollers, making them slick.  Think
of a baker's rolling pin covered in flour.  Rubbing the rollers with a clean
soft cotton cloth will liberate these troublesome particles and restore proper
operation.

  Let us know how it works out, whatever you do.

  -Frank





