I might be late to comment, but this:

In article <g1ku0v0s2vg9gil7rtjb9m30pliqh5np41@4ax.com>, Brian Inglis wrote:

>VAX interrupt handling was also really "heavy" -- benchmarks with
>the same I/O device configurations showed that 11/780s could not
>handle as many interactive users or SCADA device interrupts as
>11/70s and other I/O was no faster on the 11/780. This situation
>did not get better until terminal servers and LAT were available.

toggled my memories.

As a 3rd year student at what is now known as {astro,physik}.uni-bonn.de, 
I was a student programmer slave to some PhD student (Hi C.!) who designed
and implemented sort of an early version of USB to control vacuum pumps 
and magnets and similar devices at a new extension to the electron
accellerator. That is, the master was an 11/750, some serial daisy chained
bus operated in master-slave fashion connected to ECB bus cages with an
i8085 board and some interface hardware in them (and I had to write mostly
test software for said 8085 cpus, to check they really could control the
hardware. I think of 3 programs which I wrote, 2 found that the interface
hardware was faulty.) Anyway...

There was another 11/750 side-by-side with mine, some older equipment, 
a 11/780 (and for some time a PDP-10 of some sort) in another room, and
lots of terminals all over the building, a few connected to our machine,
lots to the 11/780. The 780 used to be known as PIB:: on HEPNET and as 
DBNPIB5 on EARN.

The 780 administrator talked one day to C and told him that his
machine would at certain times of day be nearly 100% on the interupt
stack.  C asked for the numbers of terminals connected to old serial
i/o boards (that needed one interupt per output character), did a
back of the head calculation, (N lines times 1 input char resulting in 
2k output chars per second times K microseconds per interupt) and
offered advice.

The advice was to move as many serial lines as possible to the more
intelligent serial interface boards, or to terminal servers talking LAT.

Apparently, full screen editors put some stress at output capabilities. 

Regards,
	-is


